<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thessaloniki</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Salonica, Salonika, Thessalonica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thessaloniki is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the main port of Macedonia, at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermaic-gulf">Thermaic Gulf</a>. Founded around 315 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, it was a leading city of the Roman and Byzantine empires and, under Ottoman rule from 1430, home to a large Sephardic Jewish community. It became Greek in 1912, and most of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> in 1943.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thessaloniki.jpg" alt="Thessaloniki" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tessaloniki_BW_2017-10-05_18-22-47.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Thessaloniki stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermaic-gulf">Thermaic Gulf</a>, at the northwest corner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>, bounded on the west by the delta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axios</a> and on the southeast by Mount Chortiatis. It lies 502 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a> carried trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> through the city and linked the empire with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> founded the city around 315 BCE, on or near the ancient town of Therma, and named it after his wife Thessalonike, a half-sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Under the kingdom of Macedonia it kept its own autonomy and became the most important city in Macedonia, though Pella remained the capital. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> it was the second city after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in wealth and size, with 150,000 inhabitants in the mid-12th century, until it passed to Venetian control in 1423.</p><p>Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> captured and sacked the city in 1430; contemporary reports estimated that about one-fifth of its population was enslaved. Under Ottoman rule it was one of the empire's most important trading hubs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> arriving from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> made its Jewish community mostly Sephardic. The city passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> on 8 November 1912. In 1917 a great fire destroyed much of the centre, and planners including Thomas Mawson and Ernest Hébrard rebuilt it on a plan of axes, diagonal streets and monumental squares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Thessaloniki is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the capital of the region of Central Macedonia. The municipality had 319,045 inhabitants in 2021 and the metropolitan area 1,006,112. Its Paleochristian and Byzantine monuments form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and the Ano Poli, the old town that escaped the fire, is also heritage-listed. The Roman forum, found by accident in the 1960s, is among its sites. Aristotle University, founded in 1926, is the largest university in Greece.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thessaloniki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Macedonian society was less urban than that of southern Greece, though old and new cities flourished: Pella was the royal seat, <b>Thessalonica</b> and Cassandreia were major ports, and Demetrias, founded by Demetrius Poliorcetes around 293 BCE on the Gulf of Pagasae, became a royal residence and fortress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade with Byzantium was regulated by treaties, notably one concluded in 716 that fixed the frontier and set rules for merchants, and disputes over trade could lead to war, as in 894 when Byzantium moved the Bulgarian market from Constantinople to <b>Thessalonica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The new republic governed a mountainous peninsula at the southern end of the Balkans with the longest coastline in the Mediterranean, up to 227 inhabited islands and some ten million people, its capital Athens and its co-capital <b>Thessaloniki</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They settled in new suburbs of Athens and <b>Thessaloniki</b> and on land in Macedonia distributed by agrarian reform.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>William II sent expeditions against Alexandria in 1174, which failed, and against Byzantium in 1185, which sacked <b>Thessaloniki</b> before being defeated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire claimed the rest, with vassal fiefs in Greece: the Kingdom of <b>Thessalonica</b>, the Principality of Achaea, the Duchy of Athens and the Duchy of the Archipelago.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
